{"id":"dcac3c05-5375-49b6-8b3d-cab6f2ac4bdc","object":"chat.completion","created":1784837657,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' or 'required_symbols' in case\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    req_syms = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No nodes provided\\\")\\n    # Build node map and children map\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if nid is None:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing 'id'\\\")\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        sym = n.get('symbol')\\n        depth = n.get('depth')\\n        removable = n.get('removable')\\n        utility = n.get('utility')\\n        if any(v is None for v in (sym, depth, removable, utility)):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {nid} missing required field\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        if parent is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Multiple roots\\\")\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root node\\\")\\n    # Initialize retained set with all node ids\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending (string comparison)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue  # already removed (shouldn't happen but safe)\\n        # Condition: not root, symbol not required, removable, no retained children\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in req_syms:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check children: any child still in retained?\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # All conditions pass: remove node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    # Sort retained IDs naturally (string order)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a Python implementation for `solve(case)` that performs grammar-guided node reduction. The case payload includes nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. The goal: reduce the tree by removing nodes (tentatively) while preserving required symbols and grammar validity (implied by parent-child relationships? The grammar is not given explicitly but we have parent references, so tree structure is already grammar-valid). We must not remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Process deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\n\nWe need to implement algorithm: likely a greedy reduction that iterates nodes in depth order (descending) and checks if node can be removed (removable flag, not required symbol, not root, and after removal, all required symbols still present in the tree). Also if node has children that are retained? Actually condition: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is not removed, we cannot remove it. So we process nodes from deepest to shallowest, so that children are considered before parent. That ensures that when we consider a node, its children have already been either removed or retained. So we can check: if any child's ID is in retained set, then node cannot be removed. Also check that after removal, all required symbols are still represented. But removal of a node also removes its subtree? The problem says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Probably removal means removing that node and its subtree? In parse tree reduction, removing a node typically removes its entire subtree. The conditions: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if node has children that are retained, you cannot remove it because that would remove those children. So we process from bottom up: first decide which leaf nodes to remove, then internal nodes. For each node, if removable and not root and not required symbol, and none of its children are in the retained set (i.e., all children are either removed or none exist), and after removal all required symbols still present (by checking the set of symbols from remaining nodes), then we can remove it. But removal of node also removes its entire subtree, so we need to ensure that required symbols in that subtree are still present elsewhere. So we need to check if there is another node with same symbol that is not being removed. Since we process bottom-up, we can simulate removals incrementally.\n\nSimpler approach: Build a list of nodes, sort by depth descending, then by ID ascending (tie break). Initialize retained set as all node IDs. For each node in that order, try to see if it can be removed. Conditions: node id != root id, node's symbol not in required_symbols (except root?), node.removable == True, and node has no children that are still retained (i.e., for each child in children list, child ID not in retained). Also after removal (i.e., if we remove it from retained set), the set of symbols from remaining retained nodes must still contain all required_symbols. If yes, remove it and record in removal order. Continue.\n\nBut careful: When we remove a node, its descendants are also removed (since they are children). However, if we process from deepest to shallowest, the children are already considered before parent. So if we remove a child, it's already not in retained. Then when considering parent, its children list will not contain retained children (since removed). So no issue. However, the condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" is satisfied.\n\nAlso we must ensure that after removal of a node, required symbols remain. The required symbols are listed. If a required symbol appears only in the subtree of a node we are about to remove, then removal would violate. So we need to check if the symbol appears elsewhere among the remaining nodes (excluding that subtree). But since we process bottom-up, the subtree nodes are already removed? Actually if we are removing a parent, its children might still be in retained if they haven't been considered yet (if parent depth is shallower, children are deeper and already processed). Since we process deeper first, children are already decided. If any child was not removed, it's in retained, then parent cannot be removed because node has retained children. So that case is caught. If all children were removed, then the subtree is empty. The only symbols that could be lost are the node's own symbol and any symbols of ancestors? No, ancestors are separate. So needed check: after removing node, does the set of symbols of all retained nodes (including node itself? we remove node) still contain required_symbols? Since node's own symbol might be required. So we need to check if node's symbol is in required_symbols and if it appears elsewhere. But condition \"Never remove the root, a required symbol...\" says we cannot remove a node if its symbol is required? Actually it says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node with required symbol cannot be removed at all. That is stricter than just checking representation. So we should not attempt to remove nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols (except root? root is also required but root is already excluded). So condition: node.symbol not in required_symbols. Also root.id != node.id.\n\nSo algorithm: \n1. Build mapping from node id to node dict.\n2. Build children mapping: for each node, find its children by matching parent. (case has 'parent' field).\n3. Initialize retained = set of all node ids.\n4. removal_order = [].\n5. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n6. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id not in retained: continue (already removed? not possible if we process each node once, but after removing parent, its children are already removed so they won't be in loop? Actually children are processed before parent, so they would have been considered already; if they were not removed, they remain in retained, then parent cannot be removed due to children. If they were removed, they are not in retained. So node.id is always in retained when we process it because we haven't removed it yet. But if we remove a parent, its children are already removed (so not in retained), but the parent itself is still in retained until we decide to remove it. So fine.)\n   - Check conditions:\n     a. node.id != root id (we know root id is \"root\" from fixture? But could be any? We'll find root by parent null).\n     b. node['symbol'] not in required_symbols.\n     c. node['removable'] == True.\n     d. For each child in children[node.id], if child id in retained, then cannot remove (because node has retained children). If any child is in retained, skip.\n   - If conditions pass, then tentatively remove: compute set of symbols from retained nodes excluding the subtree? But easier: After removal, we need to ensure that required_symbols are still represented. Since we are only removing this node (and its subtree which is already removed because children are not retained), the only symbol that could be missing is the node's own symbol if it is required but we already checked it's not required. However, what about a required symbol that appears in the subtree but children are removed? For children already removed, those symbols are gone. But if those symbols are required, they must appear elsewhere. Since we removed children earlier (if they were removable and conditions met), we already ensured that after each removal, required symbols remained. So by induction, at this point the current tree (retained set) still has all required symbols. Removing this node (which is not required) will not remove any required symbol because none of its children are retained, and its own symbol is not required. So condition satisfied automatically? But careful: Could there be a required symbol that appears only in the subtree of a node that we are considering, but children are already removed? Those children were removed only if they were not required and conditions met. But if a child had a required symbol, we would not have removed it because we never remove nodes with required symbol. So any required symbol node would still be in retained. If a required symbol is not a node itself but appears as a descendant? Actually symbols are attached to nodes. So if a required symbol appears in a node, that node cannot be removed. So it will be retained. So if we are considering a parent, and its child has required symbol, that child is retained, so parent cannot be removed due to retained children. So safe. Therefore, after checking children retained, we don't need to check required symbols again. However, to be robust, we can still check that after removal, the set of symbols from retained nodes (excluding the node) still contains all required_symbols. But since we never removed required symbols, it should hold. But maybe a required symbol appears only in the current node? That's covered by node.symbol not in required_symbols. So fine.\n\nThus removal condition: (node.id != root_id) and (node['symbol'] not in required_symbols) and (node['removable'] is True) and (all(child_id not in retained for child_id in children[node.id])). If true, then remove: retained.remove(node.id), removal_order.append(node.id).\n\nAfter loop, retained_ids sorted (by ID? The output should be sorted retained IDs? The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we sort retained IDs ascending (string sort? likely alphabetical but IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Usually ascending order by ID string). And removal order as list of IDs in order removed.\n\nEdge cases: What if some nodes have depth that is not consistent? We trust input. Also ensure that we don't accidentally remove root. Root is not removable (removable false) and we check node.id != root_id.\n\nNow, we must validate every required field before computing result. The case payload must have 'nodes', 'required_symbols'. Nodes list must contain objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. We should check existence. If missing, raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce JSON implementation.\n\nAlso note: \"candidate_artifact:1569\" etc. But we just need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) that returns dict with 'retained_ids' (list) and 'removal_order' (list).\n\nLet's generate code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1849,"completion_tokens":2856,"total_tokens":4705,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2228},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":57},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}